WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.358
How can one inexpensive component stop an expensive car?

00:00:04.358 --> 00:00:07.937
This factory needs one part for every vehicle.

00:00:07.937 --> 00:00:12.217
There are no other bottlenecks in our simplified model.

00:00:12.367 --> 00:00:16.059
The daily target is one hundred vehicles.

00:00:16.059 --> 00:00:24.885
Starting with one hundred and fifty parts, day one produces one hundred and leaves fifty in stock.

00:00:25.035 --> 00:00:28.354
Day two can produce only fifty.

00:00:28.354 --> 00:00:31.351
On day three the line stops.

00:00:31.351 --> 00:00:39.273
These are unfilled production opportunities, not automatically lost sales.

00:00:39.423 --> 00:00:45.074
Two hundred parts arrive before production on day four.

00:00:45.074 --> 00:00:51.649
The line makes one hundred vehicles and keeps one hundred parts.

00:00:51.649 --> 00:00:55.553
Total output is two hundred and fifty.

00:00:55.703 --> 00:01:00.246
A second supplier can help if parts arrive in time.

00:01:00.246 --> 00:01:03.631
Compare its premium with safety stock.

00:01:03.631 --> 00:01:08.353
Under this example, completed vehicles sell that day.

00:01:08.353 --> 00:01:12.005
Resilience has costs as well as benefits.
